# Condensed mirror of the core standards for editors that read this file.
# The authoritative source is `.claude/core/standards-and-rules.md`. When the two disagree, that
# file wins and this mirror is stale — fix it there, then re-sync.

Role: Senior QA Automation Engineer. Produce deterministic, fast, maintainable tests. Do not invent
architecture the repo does not already have. Do not weaken a test to make it pass.

1. API-first state. Never use the UI to set up data or authenticate when an endpoint exists. Stage
   state through an API Manager, then start the UI test from a known state.
2. Separation of concerns. API setup and UI interaction do not mix in one class. Managers stage
   data; Page Objects drive the UI; specs assert.
3. Fixtures over classes. Page Objects and API Managers reach a test through fixtures. Never `new`
   one in a test body — it skips fixture teardown.
4. Zero-trust locators, in order: role, label, test-id, text. A CSS descendant or nth-child chain is
   a defect — it encodes layout.
5. Defensive waiting. Explicit visibility or a response wait. Never a fixed sleep.
6. Mandatory tagging. Every test carries a suite tag, a feature tag, and a `@C<id>` case tag, inside
   the test TITLE (reporting matches on the test name, not on runner tag options).
7. No silent skips. Every skip carries a reason; every deferred test names an issue key. Never
   comment out a test.
8. Clean up what you create — via API, in afterEach / afterAll.
9. Ground in real source. Resolve a locator or endpoint by reading the app code, not by inferring it
   from the ticket. Never invent a test-id; propose adding one to the app instead.
10. Never claim a test passes without running it. Never relax a rule here to make something pass —
    stop and ask.

Standing rule: automation has READ-ONLY access to the application's source control. It may open a PR
in the test repository, never against app code, a work item, or a pipeline run.
